<DocsLayout title="band worktrees — Band Docs">
  <h1>band worktrees</h1>

  <p>
    The <code>band worktrees</code> command group manages worktrees within repos. A
    worktree is an isolated working environment backed by a Git worktree. Each worktree
    gets its own branch and working directory, and acts as the scope for chat panes,
    terminals, browser tabs, and cronjobs.
  </p>

  <h2>List worktrees</h2>

  <p>Display all worktrees across all repos, or filter to a single repo.</p>

  <pre><code>band worktrees list
band worktrees list my-app</code></pre>

  <p>Example output:</p>

  <pre><code>REPO     BRANCH            WORKTREE ID    PATH
my-app      fix-auth-bug      ws_abc123       /Users/dev/.band/worktrees/my-app/fix-auth-bug
my-app      add-dark-mode     ws_def456       /Users/dev/.band/worktrees/my-app/add-dark-mode
api-server  update-deps       ws_ghi789       /Users/dev/.band/worktrees/api-server/update-deps</code></pre>

  <h2>Create a worktree</h2>

  <p>
    Create a new worktree in a repo. Band will create a Git worktree with a new branch
    and (if the repo has a <code>setup</code> command in <code>.band/config.json</code>)
    run setup automatically.
  </p>

  <pre><code>band worktrees create &lt;repo&gt; &lt;branch&gt;</code></pre>

  <p>Both arguments are positional. Examples:</p>

  <pre><code>band worktrees create my-app fix-auth-bug
band worktrees create my-app add-dark-mode --base main
band worktrees create my-app review --prompt "Review this PR" --agent claude-code</code></pre>

  <p>
    The worktree is created at <code>~/.band/worktrees/&lt;repo&gt;/&lt;branch&gt;</code>
    by default (the parent directory is the configurable
    <a href="/docs/settings"><code>worktreesDir</code></a> setting). The new branch is
    created from the repo's default branch unless you pass <code>--base</code>.
  </p>

  <p>Options:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><code>repo</code> (positional, required) — name of a registered repo.</li>
    <li><code>branch</code> (positional, required) — name of the branch to create.</li>
    <li><code>--base &lt;branch&gt;</code> — base branch (defaults to the repo's default branch).</li>
    <li><code>--prompt &lt;text&gt;</code> — kick off a chat in the new worktree with this initial prompt.</li>
    <li><code>--agent &lt;id&gt;</code>, <code>--model &lt;name&gt;</code>, <code>--mode &lt;mode&gt;</code> — agent overrides for the initial prompt.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Remove a worktree</h2>

  <p>
    Remove a worktree by <code>&lt;repo&gt; &lt;branch&gt;</code>. This deletes the Git
    worktree directory, removes the worktree record from Band's database, and runs the
    repo's <code>teardown</code> command if one is configured.
  </p>

  <pre><code>band worktrees remove my-app fix-auth-bug</code></pre>

  <p>
    Running tasks in the worktree are aborted before removal. The Git branch itself is
    <em>not</em> deleted from the repo repository — you can still merge or reference it.
  </p>

  <h2>JSON output</h2>

  <pre><code>band worktrees list --output json</code></pre>

  <p>Example JSON output:</p>

  <pre><code set:html={`{
  "worktrees": [
    {
      "repo": "my-app",
      "branch": "fix-auth-bug",
      "worktreeId": "ws_abc123",
      "path": "/Users/dev/.band/worktrees/my-app/fix-auth-bug"
    }
  ]
}`}></code></pre>

  <h2>Notes</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Branch names must be unique within a repo.</li>
    <li>Each worktree has its own isolated file system via Git worktrees, so concurrent agents on different branches don't conflict.</li>
    <li>Worktrees persist across restarts — they are stored in SQLite and the worktree stays on disk.</li>
    <li>Use <a href="/docs/cli-chats"><code>band chats send</code></a> from inside the worktree directory to drive an agent.</li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
